course: dictionary
lesson: 12601
title: Spotting a change of colour
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Discolouration is a noun. It means a change in the usual colour of something, often to a worse colour. It can also mean the spot or patch where the colour has changed.
explain: Look at the clues around it: «The white sink had a yellow discolouration around the plughole.» The sink should be white, but now there is yellow, so the colour has changed. Yellow is a worse colour for a white sink, so the word fits.
explain: British, Irish, Australian and New Zealand writers spell it with a u: discolouration. American writers often spell it discoloration. It is the same word with the same meaning.
fact: Discolouration is a noun. It can mean the change of colour itself, or a spot or patch where the colour has changed.
fact: A discolouration is often a sign of damage, dirt, mould or illness.
fact: Phrases you may meet are «discolouration of the water» and «slight discolouration».
fact: A good check is to swap your meaning into the sentence: «The wall had a grey patch where the colour had changed» still makes sense.
exercise: «There was a grey discolouration on the bathroom wall where the damp had spread.» What is the discolouration? | *A spot or patch where the colour has changed | A loud noise | A new coat of paint | A small window
exercise: «The leaves showed a slight discolouration before they fell.» What does slight discolouration mean? | A large, heavy branch | *A small change in the colour of the leaves | A bright, shiny new leaf | A sound made by the wind
exercise: Ben says discolouration just means dirt. Is he right? | *Not quite, because it means a change of colour, which can be a sign of damage or illness | No, it means a bright, shiny colour | No, it means a new hat | Yes, it always means dirt on the surface
test: «The water in the pond had a brown discolouration after the storm.» What does this sentence tell you? | The pond has grown bigger | The water is now very sweet to taste | The water is now cold and clean | *The colour of the water has changed, and it may be a worse colour
test: Which phrase shows the change of colour in the water? | slight laughter | *discolouration of the water | a loud whistle | a quick walk
exam: Which sentence uses discolouration correctly? | «The discolouration sang a happy song at the party.» | *«The silver spoon had a black discolouration after it sat in the egg.» | «The sky was discolouration and the sun was loud.» | «She ran fast and discolouration jumped over the fence.»
